1043	The RR-MoA routing overhead ($+3.1$\,ms) is dominated by the sparse expert selection loop; the router itself (Conv1d + linear, ${\sim}1.1$K params) adds $<0.1$\,ms. SR-MoA dense and Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} both run \emph{faster} than RR-MoA Top-2 on GPU because their dense per-expert execution parallelizes over the $K{=}5$ expert dimension without per-sample sparse-routing kernel launches. Notably, Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} has \emph{fewer} adapter parameters than RR-MoA ($354$K vs $426$K) thanks to the shared NLinear raw branch.
1044	
1045	At deployment scale, adapter swapping between tenants requires loading $426$K parameters (${\sim}1.7$\,MB) from host RAM, orders of magnitude cheaper than reloading a $35$M parameter backbone. At $N{=}100$ tenants, adapter-swapping throughput is $141$ tenants/sec. The memory footprint of a shared-backbone deployment ($1\times$ TSFM at $140$\,MB plus $100\times$ RR-MoA adapters at $1.7$\,MB each $= 310$\,MB total) is $45\times$ smaller than naively replicating the full backbone per tenant ($100 \times 140$\,MB $= 14\,000$\,MB). \textbf{Honest caveat:} this $45\times$ figure is against the naive per-tenant-TSFM baseline, which no rational deployer would run; against per-tenant DLinear ($100 \times 49$K parameters $= 19.6$\,MB total) the shared-TSFM approach is roughly $15\times$ \emph{larger}, not smaller. The memory argument for TSFMs over DLinear is therefore not the core motivation; that role is played by cross-task reuse, zero-shot onboarding, microsecond adapter hot-swap, and the elimination of $O(N)$ per-tenant training pipelines (see \S\ref{sec:intro} and Appendix~\ref{app:deployment}).
